#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ctlhq-worktree-audit: classify every git worktree by size, merge state, uncommitted work, and PR state; never deletes.
set -u
# Resolve this file through symlinks (macOS bash 3 has no readlink -f).
src="$0"
while [ -L "$src" ]; do
	dir="$(cd "$(dirname "$src")" && pwd)"
	src="$(readlink "$src")"
	case "$src" in /*) ;; *) src="$dir/$src" ;; esac
done
here="$(cd "$(dirname "$src")" && pwd)"
scripts="$(cd "$here/../scripts" && pwd)"

usage() {
	cat <<'EOF'
ctlhq-worktree-audit: classify every git worktree by size, merge state, uncommitted work, and PR state; never deletes.

Usage: ctlhq-worktree-audit [-b|--base <ref>] [--no-fetch] [--recent-days <n>] [repo-path]

Worktree prune audit. Classifies every git worktree by size, merge state,
uncommitted work, remote/PR state, and the most recent chat that operated in
it. Emits a table sorted by size with a suggested bucket. Never deletes
anything; deletion stays a human-gated step in the playbook.

  repo-path          defaults to the current repo.
  -b <ref>           sets the ref the MERGED column checks against (e.g. origin/integration).
                     Without it the base is origin/HEAD, then origin/main, then origin/master.
                     origin/HEAD is the remote's default branch, not necessarily the branch PRs
                     merge into; pass -b when they differ.
  --no-fetch         skip the fetch of the base. By default, when the base is an origin/*
                     ref, the audit runs `git fetch origin` for it first. That uses the
                     network and writes remote-tracking refs, so a default run is not
                     read-only in that sense. The worktrees themselves are never written.
  --recent-days <n>  a chat that touched the worktree within the last n days puts it in
                     verify-recent-chat (default 4).

Columns: SIZE, AGE, MERGED, DIRTY, REMOTE, PR, LAST_CHAT, BUCKET, WORKTREE.
REMOTE: pushed, ahead<n> (local commits not on origin), behind<n> (origin commits not
local), ahead<n>-behind<m> (diverged), no-remote, or detached.
Buckets: hold-wip (tracked uncommitted edits), hold-open-pr (PR open or draft),
verify-recent-chat (a chat touched it within --recent-days), safe (its commit is in the
base, or its PR is merged), review (everything else, including a PR closed without
merge and a branch with no PR), leased (a worktree the lease command lists; never safe).
Branch rows follow the worktree rows, after a line "# branches with no worktree: <n>":
one row per local branch that no worktree has checked out, except the base's own branch
(main for origin/main). Columns: MERGED, NOT_IN_BASE (commits not in the base), BUCKET,
BRANCH, NEXT. A branch in the base is safe, and NEXT is its delete command,
`git -C <repo> branch -d <branch>`. That command checks the branch against HEAD or its
upstream, so it may refuse; it never deletes unmerged work. A branch whose pull request
merged (a squash merge, for example), and whose tip is still that pull request's head
commit, is safe too, and NEXT is `git -C <repo> branch -D <branch>`. Any other branch,
including one with commits after the merged head, is hold-unmerged, never safe. Without
gh, only a branch in the base is safe.

Lease setting. A lease manager is a separate tool that hands out reusable worktrees.
~/.ctlhq/worktree-lease.toml names its listing command in one line:
  command = "<command that prints the leased worktrees as JSON>"
Without the file, the audit reads no leases. With it, the audit runs the command once
with /bin/sh, for at most 30 seconds. On success the command exits 0 and prints:
  {"slots": [{"workspace_path": "/absolute/path", ...}, ...]}
On failure it exits nonzero and may print {"error": {"message": "<why>"}}.
A worktree at a listed workspace_path is bucket leased. When the file has no command
line, or the command times out, exits nonzero, or prints no slots array, a "warn:" line
names the error and no worktree row is safe.
The line after "# base:" is "# lease: none" without the file, "# lease: ok" after the
command ran, or "# lease: failed; no worktree row is safe".

Needs git, jq, du, rg, and python3. Uses gh for PR state when it is available.
LAST_CHAT is the newest chat that names the worktree's path, found in the Claude Code
transcript folders of the main worktree and of the worktree (~/.claude/projects/<slug>/)
and in every Codex session on this machine (~/.codex/sessions/, all workspaces, because
Codex files sessions by date, not by folder). From a match it keeps only the file's date.
It reads "-" when no chat names it.
Timestamps use BSD stat -f and date -r (macOS); GNU stat -c and date -d are the fallback.
Exit 0 after printing the table; 1 when not in a git repo, the repo path is missing or
not a git repository, or no base ref is found; 2 on a usage error.
Every error prints three lines on stderr: "ctlhq-worktree-audit: <what>", "why: <why>", and
"fix: <command>". A failed fetch of the base prints a "warn:" line and a "fix:" line, and
the table still follows.

Runs: bash skills/ctlhq-mode/scripts/worktree-audit.sh
EOF
}
for arg in "$@"; do
	case "$arg" in
	-h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
	esac
done

exec bash "$scripts/worktree-audit.sh" "$@"
